0547752\" r=\"16.733126291998992\" fill=\"transparent\" class=\"_74-EFW_nodeHit\"></circle></a><a aria-label=\"Interpretability dreams\" href=\"/research/interpretability-dreams\"><circle cx=\"31.39864\" cy=\"61.394896487842544\" r=\"16.733126291998992\" fill=\"transparent\" class=\"_74-EFW_nodeHit\"></circle></a><a aria-label=\"Softmax Linear Units\" href=\"/research/softmax-linear-units\"><circle cx=\"248.60136\" cy=\"22.32338703110865\" r=\"16.733126291998992\" fill=\"transparent\" class=\"_74-EFW_nodeHit\"></circle></a><a aria-label=\"A Mathematical Framework for Transformer Circuits\" href=\"/research/a-mathematical-framework-for-transformer-circuits\"><circle cx=\"171.58802302656136\" cy=\"82.67661296889136\" r=\"16.733126291998992\" fill=\"transparent\" class=\"_74-EFW_nodeHit\"></circle></a></svg></div></div><div class=\"tOQyXa_headline-6 yApI1G_headline\" data-component=\"Headline\">Related articles</div><ul class=\"yApI1G_list\"><li><div class=\"yApI1G_card _8J1zJG_card\"><header class=\"_8J1zJG_cardHeader\"><div class=\"_8J1zJG_eyebrowsWrapper\"><p>Interpretability</p><p>Oct 5, 2023</p></div></header><div class=\"_8J1zJG_text\" data-lines=\"3\" style=\"--text-lines:3\"><a class=\"_8J1zJG_link\" href=\"/research/decomposing-language-models-into-understandable-components\">Decomposing language models into understandable components</a></div></div></li></ul><a data-component=\"AnchorButton\" data-variant=\"tertiary\" class=\"f9ZZrG_button f9ZZrG_corner-small f9ZZrG_variant-tertiary f9ZZrG_size-small f9ZZrG_icon-format-trailing yApI1G_cta\" rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"/search?topic=interpretability\">View more<span class=\"f9ZZrG_icon\"><svg width=\"20\" height=\"20\" viewBox=\"0 0 20 20\" fill=\"currentColor\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" style=\"flex-shrink:0\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"M11.1465 4.64648C11.3417 4.45127 11.6582 4.45136 11.8535 4.64648L16.8535 9.64649L16.916 9.72267C16.9703 9.80418 17 9.90061 17 10C17 10.1326 16.9473 10.2598 16.8535 10.3535L11.8535 15.3535C11.6583 15.5486 11.3417 15.5487 11.1465 15.3535C10.9513 15.1583 10.9514 14.8418 11.1465 14.6465L15.293 10.5H3.5C3.2239 10.5 3.00006 10.2761 3 10C3 9.72387 3.22386 9.50001 3.5 9.50001H15.293L11.1465 5.35352C10.9514 5.15826 10.9513 4.8417 11.1465 4.64648Z\"></path></svg></span></a></div></aside></div><div class=\"PA4F1q_body\" data-article-body=\"true\" data-theme=\"ivory\"><h3 class=\"tOQyXa_headline-5 PA4F1q_reading-column post-section\" data-component=\"Headline\" id=\"abstract\">Abstract</h3><p class=\"PA4F1q_reading-column post-text\">In our latest paper, <a id=\"base-ui-_R_7qdrpbsnpfkq6btb_\" data-base-ui-tooltip-trigger=\"\" href=\"https://transformer-circuits.pub/2023/monosemantic-features\"><em>Towards Monosemanticity: Decomposing Language Models With Dictionary Learning</em></a>, we outline evidence that there are better units of analysis than individual neurons, and we have built machinery that lets us find these units in small transformer models. These units, called features, correspond to patterns (linear combinations) of neuron activations. This provides a path to breaking down complex neural networks into parts we can understand, and builds on previous efforts to interpret high-dimensional systems in neuroscience, machine learning, and statistics. In a transformer language model, we decompose a layer with 512 neurons into more than 4000 features which separately represent things like DNA sequences, legal language, HTTP requests, Hebrew text, nutrition statements, and much, much more. Most of these model properties are invisible when looking at the activations of individual neurons in isolation.</p></div></div></article></div><!--$--><!--/$-->","canvases":0}